        "sourceStart": 2293,
        "sourceEnd": 2303
      }
    ],
    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 10,
        "sourceEnd": 189,
        "text": "Welcome to this week's open lecture in the Hall Building. Our speaker is Dr. Lena Ortiz, a visiting lecturer in social science. The talk is called 'Why Cooperation Is Not Simple.'",
        "boundary": "section",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "47f6a691c28216f9dd543049487299be4ada978f0fd4669e9a06216fc9f40962",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 191,
        "sourceEnd": 383,
        "text": "DR. ORTIZ: Thank you. Before I start, let me correct one thing on the poster. It says I study 'teamwork.' I actually study cooperation — and those are not the same. Teamwork is one small part.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "f404a51d3ef4db6f0b6ae91a07853e5968b19d2609310078ba317bc33f991562",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 385,
        "sourceEnd": 491,
        "text": "STUDENT (Maya): Sorry — can I ask what you mean at the beginning? Why do people say cooperation is simple?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "8be04e1807c952516e6b53b6e841e9e1bbd03439ca44640fd6da193c1c398249",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 493,
        "sourceEnd": 726,
        "text": "DR. ORTIZ: Good question. Many textbooks suggest that cooperation is simple because people help when helping is cheap. I want to question that. I'll ask whether the cost is really the main reason, and what else might explain helping.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "16ad59cb6cf53a9e8f3483f3ee74a2ca8f9ad60a16f21781f1b0a2efde30f218",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 728,
        "sourceEnd": 779,
        "text": "STUDENT (Maya): So you think cost is not important?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "769ccf3653434cabf6fa109707c7abb8161cdd22a4297218050c0cc58272370c",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 781,
        "sourceEnd": 1168,
        "text": "DR. ORTIZ: No — I think cost matters. I'm saying it is not the only thing that matters. Let me give an example from a simulated study. In one classroom game, students could share notes before a test. Sharing cost time. Some students shared anyway. Why? They said, 'I think my classmate will share with me later.' That belief — that others will respond — mattered more than the time cost.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "17a14e2a2f1bfbc3c0eb1a2943b6aafceaffead2dfb6181002b80748e6a1e514",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1170,
        "sourceEnd": 1212,
        "text": "STUDENT (Tom): Wait, is that a real study?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "cc87e24d1c90aa6faad81c5db4c3124b83b5b8040554aa1a1b9d55706bc119f7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1214,
        "sourceEnd": 1418,
        "text": "DR. ORTIZ: It's a simulated example for teaching, not a published finding. Please don't cite it as research. The point is the logic: if people expect a future return, they may help even when it is costly.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "2427b48b8b5c801bdfc4c3e0b39157aef95bb29c3060d3baa81fd3599673b823",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1420,
        "sourceEnd": 1488,
        "text": "STUDENT (Tom): OK. So what would happen if nobody expected a return?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "6558072bd531489efa04fa6bb023fec2c271747e3e493e4c6e66f6675568985f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1490,
        "sourceEnd": 1677,
        "text": "DR. ORTIZ: If no one expected a return, helping would probably drop — but not to zero. Some people help because they think it is the right thing to do. That reason is different from cost.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "680bf7906eabeb94a48e570571da02c1aa102c259538fe5faab36a19bafb9c57",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1679,
        "sourceEnd": 1778,
        "text": "STUDENT (Maya): Can I check something? You said 'not to zero.' Do you mean some people always help?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "497f86562f19292f45d3c1c610da145ec22a7519b3dc7808998061ed8a614d7b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 1780,
        "sourceEnd": 2152,
        "text": "DR. ORTIZ: I mean some people help in some situations, not always. That's an important difference. Now, compare two explanations. Explanation A says: people help when the cost is low. Explanation B says: people help when they expect a return or when they have a personal rule. Which is better? I think B explains more cases, but A is simpler. Simple is not always correct.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "27432e762cd485d0feefb3edc9d11202bcead3fbd1a73e0c002b596ae4e00b4f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2154,
        "sourceEnd": 2196,
        "text": "STUDENT (Tom): So which one should we use?",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "3730cb69094841c9d01c6385f38da27bc1dc01984c608b1ec9861057c17eadd7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2198,
        "sourceEnd": 2291,
        "text": "DR. ORTIZ: Use both, and ask what each one misses. That is the habit I want you to take away.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "87c9c1321de4b2de49eb1e7906e82b90e8bfe37fb6201fa8b56f40eccdb40b74",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      },
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 2303,
        "sourceEnd": 2363,
        "text": "That's all for today. The next lecture is on Friday at four.",
        "boundary": "section",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "b2399c47b023b5f04e74ae7524b69b131c715f1fac49b781e3dc26a7fc029a9f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 1.2
      }
    ]
  },
